--------------------------------------------------Super Keyword--------------------------------------------
📌 What is super keyword?

super is a reference variable in Java.
It refers to the immediate parent class object.
Used to access parent constructors, methods, and variables.

🎯 Usage of super

Call parent constructor → super() must be the first statement in child constructor.

Call parent method → super.methodName() invokes parent’s version of an overridden method.

Access parent variables → Resolves ambiguity when child has variables with same name.

Constructor chaining → Used when parent has parameterized constructors (no default).

Differentiate overridden methods → Ensures parent logic is preserved alongside child logic.



⚡ Tricky Interview Questions & Notes

Q1: Can you use super in a static method?

❌ No. Static context has no object, so super is invalid.

Q2: Can you call both this() and super() in the same constructor?

❌ No. Only one constructor call allowed, and it must be the first statement.
✅ Indirect chaining is possible: this() calls another constructor, which then calls super().

Q3: What happens if parent has no default constructor?

Compiler inserts super(); automatically.
If parent has only parameterized constructors, you must explicitly call super(arguments).

Q4: Can super access private members of parent?

❌ No. Private members are hidden.
✅ Access only via parent’s public/protected methods.

Q5: Can you use super to call parent’s overridden method?

✅ Yes. super.methodName() calls parent’s version even if overridden in child.



